WEBVTT

NOTE SA1 Topic 8.2: Storage Spaces. Timings are estimates at 150 words a minute; re-sync after recording.

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.600
Welcome to Topic 8.

2
00:00:01.850 --> 00:00:03.050
2, Storage Spaces.

3
00:00:03.300 --> 00:00:04.500
In Topic 8.

4
00:00:04.750 --> 00:00:09.150
1 you worked with single disks and learned
RAID on paper.

5
00:00:09.400 --> 00:00:13.400
Now Windows Server does the RAID for you,
in software.

6
00:00:13.650 --> 00:00:18.850
You will learn the four layers of Storage
Spaces and the primordial pool.

7
00:00:19.100 --> 00:00:25.100
You will pick a resiliency type from the
minimum disks and the usable space it

8
00:00:25.350 --> 00:00:26.550
gives.

9
00:00:26.800 --> 00:00:31.600
You will choose thin or fixed
provisioning, build a pool, virtual disks

10
00:00:31.850 --> 00:00:37.050
and volumes in Server Manager and in
PowerShell, check their health, and plan

11
00:00:37.300 --> 00:00:40.100
what to do when a disk fails.

12
00:00:40.350 --> 00:00:42.350
Storage Spaces has four layers.

13
00:00:42.600 --> 00:00:46.200
At the bottom are physical disks, plain
blank drives.

14
00:00:46.450 --> 00:00:50.050
They do not have to be the same size.

15
00:00:50.300 --> 00:00:56.300
You group them into a storage pool, and
Windows treats all their space as one.

16
00:00:56.550 --> 00:01:00.950
From the pool you carve virtual disks,
also called storage spaces.

17
00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:08.000
Each one has its own resiliency, such as a
mirror or parity, and Windows sees it as

18
00:01:08.250 --> 00:01:09.450
an ordinary disk.

19
00:01:09.700 --> 00:01:14.900
On top you create volumes, with a drive
letter and NTFS or ReFS.

20
00:01:15.150 --> 00:01:21.150
In the lab, four 20 GB disks become
SA1Pool, with Mirror1 and Parity1 on it.

21
00:01:21.400 --> 00:01:26.200
Before you create any pool, Windows
already shows one, called the primordial

22
00:01:26.450 --> 00:01:27.650
pool.

23
00:01:27.900 --> 00:01:30.300
It is not a real pool.

24
00:01:30.550 --> 00:01:36.150
It is the list of every disk that is able
to join a pool.

25
00:01:36.400 --> 00:01:41.600
When you put disks into a new pool, they
leave the primordial pool.

26
00:01:41.850 --> 00:01:45.450
If you see no primordial pool, no disk
qualifies.

27
00:01:45.700 --> 00:01:51.700
A disk qualifies when it is blank, with no
partitions, and at least 4 GB.

28
00:01:51.950 --> 00:01:58.350
It must not hide behind a RAID controller,
so it connects through a simple host bus

29
00:01:58.600 --> 00:01:59.800
adapter.

30
00:02:00.050 --> 00:02:03.250
And a storage space cannot hold Windows
itself.

31
00:02:03.500 --> 00:02:06.700
This table is the heart of the topic.

32
00:02:06.950 --> 00:02:12.150
Simple stripes data with no protection:
one disk, full capacity, and one failure

33
00:02:12.400 --> 00:02:13.600
loses data.

34
00:02:13.850 --> 00:02:19.450
A two-way mirror keeps two copies: at
least two disks, survives one failure, and

35
00:02:19.700 --> 00:02:21.700
you get half the space.

36
00:02:21.950 --> 00:02:25.950
A three-way mirror needs five disks and
keeps a third.

37
00:02:26.200 --> 00:02:31.400
Parity needs three disks, survives one
failure and gives two thirds or more.

38
00:02:31.650 --> 00:02:35.250
Dual parity needs seven disks and survives
two failures.

39
00:02:35.500 --> 00:02:41.100
For the Server+ exam, plan capacity: raw
space times the usable share, then leave

40
00:02:41.350 --> 00:02:42.550
free space.

41
00:02:42.800 --> 00:02:48.800
When you create a virtual disk, you choose
how it takes space from the pool.

42
00:02:49.050 --> 00:02:51.850
Fixed takes the full size right away.

43
00:02:52.100 --> 00:02:58.900
Parity1 in the lab is fixed, so its 10 GB,
plus the parity, is reserved at once.

44
00:02:59.150 --> 00:03:01.150
Thin only promises the size.

45
00:03:01.400 --> 00:03:07.000
Mirror1 says 10 GB, but it takes pool
space only as data is written.

46
00:03:07.250 --> 00:03:08.850
The catch is overcommit.

47
00:03:09.100 --> 00:03:13.100
Thin disks can promise more than the pool
really has.

48
00:03:13.350 --> 00:03:19.750
Windows warns at 70 percent pool use by
default, and if the pool fills, the space

49
00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:21.200
goes offline.

50
00:03:21.450 --> 00:03:24.650
So watch free space and add disks early.

51
00:03:24.900 --> 00:03:27.300
Where does the speed come from?

52
00:03:27.550 --> 00:03:32.350
A virtual disk writes data in stripes
across several disks at once.

53
00:03:32.600 --> 00:03:37.000
The number of disks one stripe spans is
the column count.

54
00:03:37.250 --> 00:03:40.450
More columns means more disks working in
parallel.

55
00:03:40.700 --> 00:03:46.700
The amount written to each column in one
stripe is the interleave, 256 KB by

56
00:03:46.950 --> 00:03:48.150
default.

57
00:03:48.400 --> 00:03:50.800
The wizard chooses both for you.

58
00:03:51.050 --> 00:03:55.050
PowerShell can set them with dash
NumberOfColumns and dash Interleave.

59
00:03:55.300 --> 00:04:02.100
One planning rule: to grow a space later,
the pool needs free space on as many disks

60
00:04:02.350 --> 00:04:06.750
as the space has columns, times the copies
for a mirror.

61
00:04:07.000 --> 00:04:11.800
In Server Manager, open File and Storage
Services, then Volumes, then Storage

62
00:04:12.050 --> 00:04:13.250
Pools.

63
00:04:13.500 --> 00:04:15.500
The page has three tiles.

64
00:04:15.750 --> 00:04:17.750
Storage Pools lists the pools.

65
00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:22.800
Before you build anything, the only entry
is Primordial, under Storage Spaces.

66
00:04:23.050 --> 00:04:26.250
The Virtual Disks tile is empty for now.

67
00:04:26.500 --> 00:04:30.900
The Physical Disks tile lists the disks of
the selected pool.

68
00:04:31.150 --> 00:04:37.150
Identify the data disks by their size, 20
GB, never by disk number alone, because

69
00:04:37.400 --> 00:04:38.600
numbers can change.

70
00:04:38.850 --> 00:04:44.850
The Tasks menu on each tile starts the
wizards: New Storage Pool, and later New

71
00:04:45.100 --> 00:04:46.300
Virtual Disk.

72
00:04:46.550 --> 00:04:49.350
The New Storage Pool Wizard is short.

73
00:04:49.600 --> 00:04:56.000
First you name the pool, SA1Pool, and pick
the primordial pool of SRV01 as the source

74
00:04:56.250 --> 00:04:57.450
of disks.

75
00:04:57.700 --> 00:05:02.500
Then you tick the physical disks, all four
20 GB data disks.

76
00:05:02.750 --> 00:05:05.150
Each disk has an Allocation setting.

77
00:05:05.400 --> 00:05:07.400
Automatic means it carries data.

78
00:05:07.650 --> 00:05:11.650
Hot Spare means it waits unused until
another disk fails.

79
00:05:11.900 --> 00:05:15.500
In the lab you keep all four on Automatic.

80
00:05:15.750 --> 00:05:20.950
Confirm, select Create, and the four disks
leave the primordial pool and become

81
00:05:21.200 --> 00:05:22.400
SA1Pool.

82
00:05:22.650 --> 00:05:28.650
Next, select the pool and choose New
Virtual Disk from the Tasks menu of the

83
00:05:28.900 --> 00:05:30.100
Virtual Disks tile.

84
00:05:30.350 --> 00:05:31.550
Name it Mirror1.

85
00:05:31.800 --> 00:05:36.200
On the Storage Layout page you pick
Simple, Mirror or Parity.

86
00:05:36.450 --> 00:05:40.450
With only four disks, Mirror always means
a two-way mirror.

87
00:05:40.700 --> 00:05:47.100
The page that lets you choose a three-way
mirror appears only when the pool has five

88
00:05:47.350 --> 00:05:48.550
or more disks.

89
00:05:48.800 --> 00:05:52.800
Then choose Thin provisioning and a size
of 10 GB.

90
00:05:53.050 --> 00:05:59.050
On the last page, keep Create a volume
when this wizard closes ticked, so the

91
00:05:59.300 --> 00:06:01.300
next wizard opens by itself.

92
00:06:01.550 --> 00:06:06.750
The New Volume Wizard is the same one you
met in Topic 8.

93
00:06:07.000 --> 00:06:11.000
1, because a virtual disk looks like any
other disk.

94
00:06:11.250 --> 00:06:14.050
Pick SRV01 and the virtual disk Mirror1.

95
00:06:14.300 --> 00:06:15.900
Keep the maximum size.

96
00:06:16.150 --> 00:06:18.150
Assign the drive letter M.

97
00:06:18.400 --> 00:06:23.600
On File System Settings choose NTFS or
ReFS, keep the default allocation unit

98
00:06:23.850 --> 00:06:26.250
size, and type the label Mirror.

99
00:06:26.500 --> 00:06:28.500
Leave short file names off.

100
00:06:28.750 --> 00:06:33.950
When you select Create, the wizard brings
the disk online, initializes it, creates

101
00:06:34.200 --> 00:06:36.200
the partition and formats it.

102
00:06:36.450 --> 00:06:38.850
Now the same work in PowerShell.

103
00:06:39.100 --> 00:06:44.300
Get-PhysicalDisk dash CanPool true lists
only the disks that are free to pool.

104
00:06:44.550 --> 00:06:48.950
Save them in a variable, then
New-StoragePool creates SA1Pool from those

105
00:06:49.200 --> 00:06:51.600
disks in the Windows Storage subsystem.

106
00:06:51.850 --> 00:06:54.250
The pool reports Healthy and OK.

107
00:06:54.500 --> 00:06:59.700
New-VirtualDisk creates a space: give it
the pool, a name, the resiliency setting

108
00:06:59.950 --> 00:07:01.550
and the provisioning type.

109
00:07:01.800 --> 00:07:04.600
Mirror1 is a mirror, thin, 10 GB.

110
00:07:04.850 --> 00:07:07.250
Parity1 is parity, fixed, 10 GB.

111
00:07:07.500 --> 00:07:11.100
The sizes in the output depend on your
pod.

112
00:07:11.350 --> 00:07:14.550
A new virtual disk still needs a volume.

113
00:07:14.800 --> 00:07:17.200
Use the pipeline from Topic 8.

114
00:07:17.450 --> 00:07:18.650
1.

115
00:07:18.900 --> 00:07:22.900
Get-VirtualDisk finds Parity1, Get-Disk
turns it into a disk, Initialize-Disk

116
00:07:23.150 --> 00:07:28.350
makes it GPT, New-Partition gives it the
letter P, and Format-Volume formats it

117
00:07:28.600 --> 00:07:31.000
with ReFS and the label Parity.

118
00:07:31.250 --> 00:07:32.450
Then check health.

119
00:07:32.700 --> 00:07:36.700
Get-VirtualDisk shows each space with its
resiliency, provisioning, HealthStatus and

120
00:07:36.950 --> 00:07:38.150
OperationalStatus.

121
00:07:38.400 --> 00:07:41.200
Healthy and OK are what you want.

122
00:07:41.450 --> 00:07:44.250
Get-StoragePool shows the same for the
pool.

123
00:07:44.500 --> 00:07:48.500
There is also New-Volume, which does
everything in one step.

124
00:07:48.750 --> 00:07:51.150
What happens when a disk fails?

125
00:07:51.400 --> 00:07:55.000
The virtual disk shows HealthStatus
Warning and OperationalStatus Degraded,

126
00:07:55.250 --> 00:07:57.650
but your data is still there.

127
00:07:57.900 --> 00:08:00.300
Now follow the steps in order.

128
00:08:00.550 --> 00:08:06.150
Add a new disk to the pool, or let a hot
spare step in.

129
00:08:06.400 --> 00:08:10.400
Retire the failed disk with
Set-PhysicalDisk dash Usage Retired, so

130
00:08:10.650 --> 00:08:12.650
nothing new lands on it.

131
00:08:12.900 --> 00:08:17.700
Run Repair-VirtualDisk and wait for the
job, which you can watch with

132
00:08:17.950 --> 00:08:19.150
Get-StorageJob.

133
00:08:19.400 --> 00:08:22.600
Only then remove the disk and pull it.

134
00:08:22.850 --> 00:08:25.250
A repair needs free pool space.

135
00:08:25.500 --> 00:08:28.300
In the pod, this is concept only.

136
00:08:28.550 --> 00:08:32.550
How does Storage Spaces compare with a
hardware RAID controller?

137
00:08:32.800 --> 00:08:38.800
Storage Spaces runs in Windows, takes
disks of any size and lets you add more

138
00:08:39.050 --> 00:08:41.450
later, with thin provisioning and tiers.

139
00:08:41.700 --> 00:08:46.900
Hardware RAID runs on a controller card,
usually wants matching disks, and often

140
00:08:47.150 --> 00:08:49.550
has a battery-backed or flash-backed
cache.

141
00:08:49.800 --> 00:08:53.800
Hardware RAID can hold the boot disk;
Storage Spaces cannot.

142
00:08:54.050 --> 00:08:57.650
Do not stack them: Storage Spaces wants
plain disks.

143
00:08:57.900 --> 00:09:04.700
A common design is two disks in hardware
RAID 1 for Windows, and the data disks in

144
00:09:04.950 --> 00:09:06.150
Storage Spaces.

145
00:09:06.400 --> 00:09:08.800
Two more ideas, as concepts only.

146
00:09:09.050 --> 00:09:14.250
Storage tiers mix fast SSDs and slower
hard disks in one virtual disk.

147
00:09:14.500 --> 00:09:20.500
Windows moves busy data to the SSD tier
and quiet data to the hard disks.

148
00:09:20.750 --> 00:09:22.350
Tiers need fixed provisioning.

149
00:09:22.600 --> 00:09:24.600
Storage Spaces Direct goes further.

150
00:09:24.850 --> 00:09:30.850
A failover cluster of 2 to 16 servers
pools the local drives of every server

151
00:09:31.100 --> 00:09:35.100
over the network, so storage survives a
whole server failing.

152
00:09:35.350 --> 00:09:39.750
It needs Datacenter edition and is the
base of Azure Local.

153
00:09:40.000 --> 00:09:43.600
The AZ-802 exam covers it; our
single-server pod cannot.

154
00:09:43.850 --> 00:09:45.050
Four common mistakes.

155
00:09:45.300 --> 00:09:47.300
One: picking disks by number.

156
00:09:47.550 --> 00:09:52.750
Numbers change, so pick by size and blank
state, or let Get-PhysicalDisk dash

157
00:09:53.000 --> 00:09:54.200
CanPool true choose.

158
00:09:54.450 --> 00:09:57.650
Two: using thin disks without watching the
pool.

159
00:09:57.900 --> 00:10:03.500
They can overcommit it, so watch free
space and add disks before it fills.

160
00:10:03.750 --> 00:10:05.750
Three: parity for busy servers.

161
00:10:06.000 --> 00:10:12.000
Parity writes are slow, so use a mirror
for virtual machines and busy shares, and

162
00:10:12.250 --> 00:10:14.250
parity for archive and backup.

163
00:10:14.500 --> 00:10:16.900
Four: pulling a failed disk first.

164
00:10:17.150 --> 00:10:21.150
Add, retire, repair and wait, and only
then remove it.

165
00:10:21.400 --> 00:10:23.000
Time to check yourself.

166
00:10:23.250 --> 00:10:25.650
SA1Pool has four 20 GB disks.

167
00:10:25.900 --> 00:10:31.900
First, about how much data fits in one
two-way mirror that uses the whole pool?

168
00:10:32.150 --> 00:10:36.950
Second, why does the wizard not offer a
three-way mirror for SA1Pool?

169
00:10:37.200 --> 00:10:40.000
Third, Mirror1 is thin and 10 GB.

170
00:10:40.250 --> 00:10:45.850
Why does the pool show much less used
space than 10 GB times two?

171
00:10:46.100 --> 00:10:49.300
Fourth, Get-VirtualDisk shows Mirror1 as
Warning and Degraded.

172
00:10:49.550 --> 00:10:52.350
What happened, and what do you do?

173
00:10:52.600 --> 00:10:57.000
Pause the video and answer all four before
you go on.

174
00:10:57.250 --> 00:10:58.850
Here are the answers.

175
00:10:59.100 --> 00:11:00.700
One: about 40 GB.

176
00:11:00.950 --> 00:11:07.350
A two-way mirror stores everything twice,
so 80 GB of raw space gives half, minus a

177
00:11:07.600 --> 00:11:09.200
little for pool metadata.

178
00:11:09.450 --> 00:11:14.650
Two: a three-way mirror needs at least
five disks, and SA1Pool has four.

179
00:11:14.900 --> 00:11:21.700
Three: a thin disk takes pool space only
as data is written, so an empty one uses

180
00:11:21.950 --> 00:11:23.150
almost nothing.

181
00:11:23.400 --> 00:11:27.400
Four: a disk failed, but the data is still
there.

182
00:11:27.650 --> 00:11:33.650
Add a new disk, retire the failed one, run
Repair-VirtualDisk, wait for it to finish,

183
00:11:33.900 --> 00:11:36.300
and then remove the old disk.

184
00:11:36.550 --> 00:11:38.950
Let me show you on SRV01.

185
00:11:39.200 --> 00:11:45.200
Under Disks you see the four blank 20 GB
disks, and under Storage Pools the

186
00:11:45.450 --> 00:11:46.650
primordial pool.

187
00:11:46.900 --> 00:11:52.900
I run the New Storage Pool Wizard, name it
SA1Pool and tick all four disks.

188
00:11:53.150 --> 00:11:58.750
Then the New Virtual Disk Wizard makes
Mirror1: Mirror layout, notice there is no

189
00:11:59.000 --> 00:12:01.000
three-way choice, thin, 10 GB.

190
00:12:01.250 --> 00:12:05.650
The New Volume Wizard gives it M and the
label Mirror.

191
00:12:05.900 --> 00:12:09.900
In PowerShell, New-VirtualDisk makes
Parity1, fixed, and the disk pipeline

192
00:12:10.150 --> 00:12:12.550
formats it as P with ReFS.

193
00:12:12.800 --> 00:12:16.000
Finally Get-VirtualDisk shows both spaces
Healthy and OK.

194
00:12:16.250 --> 00:12:17.450
To wrap up.

195
00:12:17.700 --> 00:12:24.100
Blank disks go into a pool, virtual disks
or spaces are carved from it, and volumes

196
00:12:24.350 --> 00:12:25.550
sit on top.

197
00:12:25.800 --> 00:12:31.000
A two-way mirror needs two disks, a
three-way mirror five, parity three and

198
00:12:31.250 --> 00:12:32.450
dual parity seven.

199
00:12:32.700 --> 00:12:38.700
Usable space is raw space times the usable
share, and you keep free space for

200
00:12:38.950 --> 00:12:40.150
repairs.

201
00:12:40.400 --> 00:12:44.800
Thin grows as it is used; fixed takes its
space now.

202
00:12:45.050 --> 00:12:49.850
Check HealthStatus and OperationalStatus,
and when a disk fails, retire it, then

203
00:12:50.100 --> 00:12:51.300
repair.

204
00:12:51.550 --> 00:12:52.750
In Lab 8.

205
00:12:53.000 --> 00:12:57.800
2 you build all of this on SRV01 in about
70 minutes.
